1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,127
Here is a test. It runs. It passes. Green, every time.

2
00:00:04,677 --> 00:00:09,199
And it tests nothing. It never checks the answer, so the bug sails straight through.

3
00:00:09,749 --> 00:00:12,527
A test is only worth something if it can fail,

4
00:00:12,527 --> 00:00:13,916
when the code is wrong.

5
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:17,237
Today you learn the five tools that make a test honest.

6
00:00:17,787 --> 00:00:18,553
Assertions,

7
00:00:18,553 --> 00:00:19,179
fixtures,

8
00:00:19,179 --> 00:00:19,596
mocks,

9
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:20,292
test data,

10
00:00:20,292 --> 00:00:21,266
and isolation.

11
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:24,187
Then you prove your tests catch real bugs.

12
00:00:25,737 --> 00:00:27,166
Here is what to expect.

13
00:00:27,716 --> 00:00:32,474
One: assertions. The claim a test makes, and why a good failure message matters.

14
00:00:33,024 --> 00:00:37,994
Two: fixtures. Setup that every test gets fresh, so you do not repeat yourself.

15
00:00:38,544 --> 00:00:43,984
Three: mocks. A safe stand in, for the parts a test must not touch, like real e-mail.

16
00:00:44,534 --> 00:00:46,563
Four: test data and isolation.

17
00:00:46,563 --> 00:00:48,254
Many cases from one test,

18
00:00:48,254 --> 00:00:50,892
and tests that never affect each other.

19
00:00:51,442 --> 00:00:53,356
Then a live demo with pie test,

20
00:00:53,356 --> 00:00:54,529
building six tests,

21
00:00:54,529 --> 00:00:57,986
and breaking the code on purpose to watch them catch it.

22
00:00:57,986 --> 00:00:59,591
And a lab with six checks.

23
00:01:01,641 --> 00:01:03,838
Assertions. The heart of every test.

24
00:01:04,388 --> 00:01:07,038
An assertion is a claim that must be true.

25
00:01:07,038 --> 00:01:07,480
Assert,

26
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:08,679
then an expression.

27
00:01:08,679 --> 00:01:09,562
If it is true,

28
00:01:09,562 --> 00:01:10,634
the test goes on.

29
00:01:10,634 --> 00:01:11,581
If it is false,

30
00:01:11,581 --> 00:01:12,527
the test fails,

31
00:01:12,527 --> 00:01:13,284
right there.

32
00:01:13,834 --> 00:01:16,619
Pie test shows you both sides when it fails.

33
00:01:16,619 --> 00:01:17,632
Not just failed,

34
00:01:17,632 --> 00:01:20,543
but: the shop gave one thousand seventy-eight,

35
00:01:20,543 --> 00:01:23,201
and we expected one thousand seventy-nine.

36
00:01:23,201 --> 00:01:25,226
That message is your first clue.

37
00:01:25,776 --> 00:01:27,908
Floating point numbers need care.

38
00:01:27,908 --> 00:01:31,267
Point one plus point two is not exactly point three.

39
00:01:31,267 --> 00:01:32,817
Use pie test dot approx,

40
00:01:32,817 --> 00:01:35,014
to compare with a small tolerance.

41
00:01:35,564 --> 00:01:38,444
And sometimes the right behavior is an error.

42
00:01:38,444 --> 00:01:42,156
Pie test dot raises says: this code must raise this error.

43
00:01:42,156 --> 00:01:43,500
If it does not raise,

44
00:01:43,500 --> 00:01:44,460
the test fails.

45
00:01:45,010 --> 00:01:49,618
One test, one idea. A test with ten assertions hides which one broke.

46
00:01:51,168 --> 00:01:53,835
Fixtures. Setup, done once, shared by name.

47
00:01:54,385 --> 00:01:58,018
Many tests need the same starting point: a fresh cart,

48
00:01:58,018 --> 00:01:58,758
a database,

49
00:01:58,758 --> 00:01:59,902
a logged in user.

50
00:01:59,902 --> 00:02:03,400
Copying that setup into every test is slow to write,

51
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:04,881
and easy to get wrong.

52
00:02:05,431 --> 00:02:07,026
A fixture is a function,

53
00:02:07,026 --> 00:02:09,220
marked with pie test dot fixture.

54
00:02:09,220 --> 00:02:10,483
A test asks for it,

55
00:02:10,483 --> 00:02:12,677
just by naming it as a parameter.

56
00:02:12,677 --> 00:02:13,941
Pie test builds it,

57
00:02:13,941 --> 00:02:15,137
and hands it over.

58
00:02:15,687 --> 00:02:16,361
By default,

59
00:02:16,361 --> 00:02:18,322
every test gets a brand new one.

60
00:02:18,322 --> 00:02:21,938
That is the point: no test leaves a mess for the next test.

61
00:02:22,488 --> 00:02:24,575
Scope can widen that: function,

62
00:02:24,575 --> 00:02:25,046
module,

63
00:02:25,046 --> 00:02:25,786
or session.

64
00:02:25,786 --> 00:02:29,623
A database that costs a second to create might be shared.

65
00:02:29,623 --> 00:02:31,171
A cart should never be.

66
00:02:31,721 --> 00:02:35,527
Shared fixtures live in a file called con F test dot pie.

67
00:02:35,527 --> 00:02:38,399
Every test file in the folder can use them,

68
00:02:38,399 --> 00:02:39,401
with no import.

69
00:02:40,951 --> 00:02:44,087
Mocks. A stand in, for what a test must not touch.

70
00:02:44,637 --> 00:02:46,927
Our shop sends a receipt by e-mail.

71
00:02:46,927 --> 00:02:47,581
In a test,

72
00:02:47,581 --> 00:02:50,263
that must never reach a real mail server.

73
00:02:50,263 --> 00:02:50,983
It is slow,

74
00:02:50,983 --> 00:02:53,992
it can fail for reasons that are not your bug,

75
00:02:53,992 --> 00:02:56,413
and you might e-mail a real customer.

76
00:02:56,963 --> 00:03:00,161
A mock is a fake object that records how it was used.

77
00:03:00,161 --> 00:03:01,609
You give it to the code,

78
00:03:01,609 --> 00:03:03,299
in place of the real mailer.

79
00:03:03,849 --> 00:03:05,832
Then you check the conversation.

80
00:03:05,832 --> 00:03:06,823
Was send called?

81
00:03:06,823 --> 00:03:07,753
How many times?

82
00:03:07,753 --> 00:03:08,930
With which address?

83
00:03:08,930 --> 00:03:13,577
That is how you test a promise like: the customer is e-mailed exactly once.

84
00:03:14,127 --> 00:03:16,303
Mock the edges of your system: mail,

85
00:03:16,303 --> 00:03:16,847
payments,

86
00:03:16,847 --> 00:03:17,451
the clock,

87
00:03:17,451 --> 00:03:18,177
the network.

88
00:03:18,177 --> 00:03:20,836
Do not mock the code you are trying to test,

89
00:03:20,836 --> 00:03:22,106
or you test the mock.

90
00:03:22,656 --> 00:03:26,474
In a later lesson, we mock a real service for the CloudCart tests.

91
00:03:28,024 --> 00:03:29,773
Test data, and isolation.

92
00:03:30,323 --> 00:03:31,536
Test data: small,

93
00:03:31,536 --> 00:03:31,963
clear,

94
00:03:31,963 --> 00:03:33,532
and chosen on purpose.

95
00:03:33,532 --> 00:03:35,529
One test can run many cases,

96
00:03:35,529 --> 00:03:37,668
with pie test dot parametrize.

97
00:03:37,668 --> 00:03:38,881
A list of inputs,

98
00:03:38,881 --> 00:03:40,663
and the expected answers.

99
00:03:40,663 --> 00:03:43,017
Each case is reported separately.

100
00:03:43,567 --> 00:03:45,738
Good data includes the edges: zero,

101
00:03:45,738 --> 00:03:46,855
the biggest value,

102
00:03:46,855 --> 00:03:47,661
the smallest,

103
00:03:47,661 --> 00:03:50,329
and the odd one that once broke production.

104
00:03:50,879 --> 00:03:53,166
Isolation: a test must pass alone,

105
00:03:53,166 --> 00:03:54,040
in any order,

106
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:54,779
on any day.

107
00:03:54,779 --> 00:03:57,335
If test two only works after test one,

108
00:03:57,335 --> 00:03:59,285
you have a hidden dependency.

109
00:03:59,835 --> 00:04:03,234
How to stay isolated: build fresh state in a fixture.

110
00:04:03,234 --> 00:04:04,709
Use a temporary folder,

111
00:04:04,709 --> 00:04:05,799
called temp path,

112
00:04:05,799 --> 00:04:06,440
for files.

113
00:04:06,440 --> 00:04:08,813
Use monkey patch to change a setting,

114
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:10,224
and put it back after.

115
00:04:10,774 --> 00:04:13,566
And a simple shape for every test: arrange,

116
00:04:13,566 --> 00:04:13,826
act,

117
00:04:13,826 --> 00:04:14,280
assert.

118
00:04:14,280 --> 00:04:14,800
Prepare,

119
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:15,644
do one thing,

120
00:04:15,644 --> 00:04:16,747
check the result.

121
00:04:18,297 --> 00:04:23,417
Go to the lab folder.

122
00:04:24,967 --> 00:04:26,866
Look around.

123
00:04:27,416 --> 00:04:27,987
The shop,

124
00:04:27,987 --> 00:04:29,258
a shared setup file,

125
00:04:29,258 --> 00:04:30,465
and a tests folder.

126
00:04:30,465 --> 00:04:33,069
The tests file is empty: we write it now.

127
00:04:33,619 --> 00:04:38,931
The code.

128
00:04:39,481 --> 00:04:41,795
The tax function: the total with tax,

129
00:04:41,795 --> 00:04:43,546
rounded to the nearest cent.

130
00:04:43,546 --> 00:04:44,984
And the discount tiers.

131
00:04:44,984 --> 00:04:49,486
The bug switch near the top is how the judge breaks the code on purpose.

132
00:04:50,036 --> 00:04:54,495
First assertion.

133
00:05:03,145 --> 00:05:04,186
Nine ninety-nine,

134
00:05:04,186 --> 00:05:05,595
with eight percent tax,

135
00:05:05,595 --> 00:05:08,351
is one thousand seventy-eight point nine two.

136
00:05:08,351 --> 00:05:11,230
To the nearest cent: one thousand seventy-nine.

137
00:05:11,780 --> 00:05:14,170
Run it.

138
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:15,356
One passed.

139
00:05:15,356 --> 00:05:17,729
But does this test really check anything?

140
00:05:17,729 --> 00:05:19,002
Let us break the code,

141
00:05:19,002 --> 00:05:20,160
with the bug switch.

142
00:05:20,710 --> 00:05:23,760
Break it.

143
00:05:24,710 --> 00:05:25,136
Failed.

144
00:05:25,136 --> 00:05:28,419
The test noticed that the code now cuts the cents off.

145
00:05:28,419 --> 00:05:31,580
A test that can fail is a test that means something.

146
00:05:32,130 --> 00:05:37,506
Add the mock import.

147
00:05:38,956 --> 00:05:46,700
The mock test.

148
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:01,736
No real mail is ever sent. The mock just counts.

149
00:06:02,286 --> 00:06:05,209
Run both.

150
00:06:05,759 --> 00:06:06,655
Two passed.

151
00:06:07,205 --> 00:06:11,941
Break the mailer.

152
00:06:12,491 --> 00:06:18,464
An F, and one failed. The customer would have got two e-mails, and the mock caught it.

153
00:06:19,014 --> 00:06:24,476
Test data.

154
00:06:39,826 --> 00:06:43,900
See each case.

155
00:06:44,450 --> 00:06:49,357
Four cases, four lines, each with its own name. If one case breaks, you see which.

156
00:06:49,907 --> 00:06:53,107
Break a tier.

157
00:06:56,557 --> 00:07:04,237
Only the gold case failed. The other three still pass. Parametrize pinpoints the bad case.

158
00:07:04,787 --> 00:07:09,224
A fixture.

159
00:07:14,474 --> 00:07:19,125
Use it.

160
00:07:32,475 --> 00:07:35,248
Run everything.

161
00:07:35,798 --> 00:07:36,758
Eight passed.

162
00:07:37,308 --> 00:07:42,002
Shared state.

163
00:07:42,552 --> 00:07:47,001
One test failed: it found items left behind by another test.

164
00:07:47,001 --> 00:07:50,930
Tests that share state fail depending on their order.

165
00:07:50,930 --> 00:07:51,820
The fixture,

166
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:53,896
with a fresh cart each time,

167
00:07:53,896 --> 00:07:56,269
is how a good suite avoids that.

168
00:07:56,819 --> 00:08:02,771
An expected error.

169
00:08:09,521 --> 00:08:12,017
Run.

170
00:08:12,567 --> 00:08:13,527
Nine passed.

171
00:08:14,077 --> 00:08:18,728
Break it.

172
00:08:19,278 --> 00:08:20,918
Failed: the cart accepted zero,

173
00:08:20,918 --> 00:08:21,871
and did not raise.

174
00:08:21,871 --> 00:08:23,035
Five bugs switched on,

175
00:08:23,035 --> 00:08:24,782
and five times the tests noticed.

176
00:08:25,332 --> 00:08:27,700
Run the judge.

177
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:31,883
The judge does the same as we did: it switches each bug on,

178
00:08:31,883 --> 00:08:33,854
and checks that your tests fail.

179
00:08:33,854 --> 00:08:34,778
Six out of six.

180
00:08:35,328 --> 00:08:37,290
Clean up.

181
00:08:37,840 --> 00:08:39,696
Removed. Nothing left behind.

182
00:08:41,246 --> 00:08:44,894
Your lab task: write tests that catch bugs. Six checks.

183
00:08:45,444 --> 00:08:46,022
One: tax,

184
00:08:46,022 --> 00:08:48,205
with a test that catches rounding.

185
00:08:48,205 --> 00:08:49,425
Two: a mock mailer,

186
00:08:49,425 --> 00:08:51,801
that proves the receipt is sent once.

187
00:08:52,351 --> 00:08:54,643
Three: parametrized discount cases,

188
00:08:54,643 --> 00:08:56,018
at least three tiers.

189
00:08:56,018 --> 00:08:58,179
Four: a fixture for a fresh cart,

190
00:08:58,179 --> 00:08:59,882
and two tests that use it.

191
00:09:00,432 --> 00:09:03,653
Five: pie test dot raises, for a bad quantity.

192
00:09:04,203 --> 00:09:05,190
Then lab check,

193
00:09:05,190 --> 00:09:06,571
until six out of six.

194
00:09:06,571 --> 00:09:08,872
A good test passes on correct code,

195
00:09:08,872 --> 00:09:11,371
and fails when its bug is switched on.

196
00:09:13,421 --> 00:09:16,216
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

197
00:09:16,766 --> 00:09:18,230
One: the repository,

198
00:09:18,230 --> 00:09:22,550
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

199
00:09:22,550 --> 00:09:24,894
in the folder academy slash lab.

200
00:09:25,444 --> 00:09:31,524
Two: the files, lab one thirty-six dot S H, and shortcuts one thirty-six dot S H.

201
00:09:32,074 --> 00:09:37,535
Three: install it, with sudo install, into slash user slash local slash S bin.

202
00:09:38,085 --> 00:09:39,921
Four: load the short names,

203
00:09:39,921 --> 00:09:42,980
with source shortcuts one thirty-six dot S H.

204
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:43,796
Then lab up,

205
00:09:43,796 --> 00:09:44,476
lab check,

206
00:09:44,476 --> 00:09:45,360
and lab down.

207
00:09:45,910 --> 00:09:48,913
Five: you need python three and pie test.

208
00:09:48,913 --> 00:09:49,645
On Ubuntu,

209
00:09:49,645 --> 00:09:50,890
sudo apt install,

210
00:09:50,890 --> 00:09:51,842
python three,

211
00:09:51,842 --> 00:09:52,502
pie test.

212
00:09:53,052 --> 00:09:56,892
Six: only on your own machine. Lab down removes everything.

213
00:09:57,442 --> 00:09:59,084
The link is in the description.

214
00:10:01,134 --> 00:10:04,782
Pass or fail rubric. Six points. All six to pass.

215
00:10:05,332 --> 00:10:08,532
One: six tests or more, all passing on correct code.

216
00:10:09,082 --> 00:10:11,599
Two: the tests catch the tax rounding bug.

217
00:10:12,149 --> 00:10:14,560
Three: the mock catches the double e-mail.

218
00:10:15,110 --> 00:10:18,715
Four: three or more parametrized cases catch the bad tier.

219
00:10:19,265 --> 00:10:22,295
Five: a fixture, and the tests catch shared state.

220
00:10:22,845 --> 00:10:26,023
Six: pie test dot raises catches the missing error.

221
00:10:26,573 --> 00:10:27,875
Pass means all six.

222
00:10:29,925 --> 00:10:31,696
Your turn, on your own machine.

223
00:10:32,246 --> 00:10:35,872
One: lab up, and open the instructions file, to learn the rules.

224
00:10:36,422 --> 00:10:40,945
Two: write the tax test, and the mock test. Break the code, and watch them fail.

225
00:10:41,495 --> 00:10:44,076
Three: add parametrize for the discount tiers.

226
00:10:44,626 --> 00:10:48,466
Four: write the fixture, and the cart tests. Then the raises test.

227
00:10:49,016 --> 00:10:52,494
Five: lab check, until six out of six. Then lab down.

228
00:10:53,044 --> 00:10:54,878
Pause the video, and begin now.

229
00:11:10,428 --> 00:11:11,303
Quick check.

230
00:11:11,853 --> 00:11:13,687
Why must a test be able to fail?

231
00:11:14,237 --> 00:11:16,093
What does a fixture give every test?

232
00:11:16,643 --> 00:11:18,030
What do you check on a mock?

233
00:11:18,580 --> 00:11:20,052
What does isolation mean?

234
00:11:20,602 --> 00:11:23,016
Objective covered: M C fifteen,

235
00:11:23,016 --> 00:11:24,106
objective two.

236
00:11:24,106 --> 00:11:25,586
Explain assertions,

237
00:11:25,586 --> 00:11:26,287
fixtures,

238
00:11:26,287 --> 00:11:26,754
mocks,

239
00:11:26,754 --> 00:11:27,533
test data,

240
00:11:27,533 --> 00:11:28,623
and isolation.

241
00:11:29,173 --> 00:11:31,243
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

242
00:11:41,793 --> 00:11:45,163
Next: run tests, and read reports and exit codes.

243
00:11:45,713 --> 00:11:49,041
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
